ada: tracebacks on RISC-V Linux via the GCC unwinder

tracebak.c has no RISC-V branch, so riscv64-linux falls through to the stub
__gnat_backtrace that records no frames: GNAT.Traceback.Call_Chain returns
an empty array and every Symbolic_Traceback / -bargs -E exception traceback
is empty -- on any libc, not just musl. Use the generic _Unwind_Backtrace
driven implementation (USE_GCC_UNWINDER), as aarch64 and ia64 Linux do;
riscv64 code carries DWARF CFI (.eh_frame) by default.

PC_ADJUST is -2: with the C extension the shortest call is the 2-byte
c.jalr, so return address - 2 always lies inside the call instruction
(the same reasoning as ARM Thumb's -2).

Found by tests/ada/tracesym under qemu-riscv64 (r5 gate tightening).
Upstream candidate (gcc bugzilla, component ada).

--- a/gcc/ada/tracebak.c	2026-09-24 19:33:46.305456215 +0200
+++ b/gcc/ada/tracebak.c	2026-09-24 19:33:46.321683613 +0200
@@ -572,6 +572,15 @@
 #define USE_GCC_UNWINDER
 #define PC_ADJUST -4
 
+/*--------------------------- RISC-V Linux ---------------------------*/
+
+#elif defined (__riscv) && defined (__linux__)
+
+#define USE_GCC_UNWINDER
+/* The shortest call instruction is the 2-byte c.jalr of the C extension,
+   so backing up 2 bytes always lands inside the call.  */
+#define PC_ADJUST -2
+
 /*----------------------------- ia64 ---------------------------------*/
 
 #elif defined (__ia64__) && (defined (__linux__) || defined (__hpux__))
